The Constitutional Council consists of the following members:

(a) the Prime Minister;  
(b) the Speaker;  
(c) the Leader of the Opposition in Parliament;  
(d) one Member of Parliament appointed by the President;  
(e) five persons appointed by the President, upon being nominated as follows:  
   (i) one Member of Parliament nominated by agreement of the majority of the Members of Parliament representing the Government;  
   (ii) one Member of Parliament nominated by agreement of the majority of the Members of Parliament of the political party or independent group to which the Leader of the Opposition belongs;  
   (iii) three persons nominated by the Speaker by agreement of the Prime Minister and the Leader of the Opposition;  
(f) one Member of Parliament nominated by agreement of the Members of Parliament other than those representing the Government and those belonging to the political party or independent group to which the Leader of the Opposition belongs, and appointed by the President.

The Speaker serves as the Chairman of the Council.

According to section 58, the Court of Appeal has the following powers upon hearing a criminal appeal:

(a) In a criminal case, the Court may:
(i) Affirm the judgment, conviction, sentence, or final order appealed from and dismiss the appeal;
(ii) Allow the appeal, set aside the judgment, conviction, sentence, or final order appealed from, and order a retrial, acquit the appellant, or make such other order as may be appropriate;
(iii) Vary the judgment, conviction, sentence, or final order appealed from, or reduce or enhance the sentence or the nature thereof, provided that where the sentence is enhanced, it shall not exceed the sentence which a Primary Court could impose;
(iv) Reverse an order of acquittal and direct a new trial.

A Primary Court may decide not to impose punishment if it finds the charge proved but considers factors such as the offender's character, background, age, health, mental condition, or circumstances of the offence. Instead of proceeding to conviction, the court can take several actions: 

(a) admonish the offender; 
(b) order payment of State costs up to fifty rupees; 
(c) order compensation; 
(d) conditionally discharge the offender with a recognizance to maintain good behavior and appear for sentencing if required, for a period not exceeding eighteen months; 
(e) apply the Probation of Offenders Ordinance. 

These actions are aimed at addressing the offender's situation without imposing a formal conviction.

A recognizance under section 18 may contain conditions regarding the following matters, as ordered by the court based on the circumstances of the case:

(a) Prohibiting the offender from associating with thieves and undesirable persons, or from frequenting undesirable places;
(b) Abstention from intoxicating liquor, particularly if the offence was related to drunkenness or committed under the influence of drink;
(c) Ensuring that the offender leads an honest and industrious life;
(d) Requiring the offender, along with their surety or sureties, to appear in chambers before the Judge of the Primary Court at specified intervals;
(e) Directing the payment of compensation and State costs as outlined in sections 16 and 18.

When making a determination and order under section 72, the court must consider the following factors:

(a) The information filed and the affidavits and documents furnished;
(b) Any other evidence on matters arising from the affidavits or documents that the court permits to be led;
(c) Oral or written submissions that may be permitted by the Judge of the Primary Court at his discretion.

